            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A committed follower, learner, or apprentice of Jesus Christ, generally understood to encompass any true Christian believer whose life is characterized by learning and obedience.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A follower who embraces the full cost of following the Master and continues until they are perfected, having the law sealed within them and receiving His Name.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A learner and follower of Jesus.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A follower who continues under the Master to the point of perfection and inner sealing.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus broadly equates discipleship with basic Christian identity (any true believer). The book restricts the definition to a subset of followers who pay the full cost, persist until the law is sealed within them, and achieve the state of being perfected as the Master.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book rejects the common view that any believer who has entered by faith and is merely growing constitutes a complete 'disciple,' arguing instead that true discipleship culminates in a specific attainment of perfection and sealing.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book maintains the core consensus definition of a disciple as a follower and learner under a Master, but significantly narrows the extension by applying a rigorous standard of perfection, sealing, and fully accepting the cost (linking it to Revelation 14:1 and Isaiah 8:16).\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Book of Acts frequently uses the term 'disciple' as a simple synonym for 'believer' or 'Christian' without implying that all those individuals had already attained perfection or the complete sealing of the law within them.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
